/**
\addtogroup engine

The `laplace::engine` module represents structured distributional regression models as flat device-accessible arrays, evaluates per-unit parameter tensors and their derivatives up to order 4 inside Kokkos kernels, and exposes both inner objectives (for smooth or kinked penalties from \ref penalty "laplace::penalty") and the Laplace marginal criterion with its exact gradient and Hessian to the library's optimizers. Include `<laplace/engine/row_design.hpp>`, `<laplace/engine/pointwise_node.hpp>`, `<laplace/engine/predictor_tensor.hpp>`, `<laplace/engine/row_model.hpp>`, `<laplace/engine/row_kernels.hpp>`, `<laplace/engine/penalty_term.hpp>`, `<laplace/engine/inner_fit.hpp>`, `<laplace/engine/outer_rows.hpp>`, or `<laplace/engine/marginal_criterion.hpp>`.

The functions marked `LAPLACE_HD` (`pointwise_node::n_inputs()`, `logistic_curve_value()`, `logistic_curve_d1()` to `logistic_curve_d4()`, `packed_pair()`, `packed_triple()`, `packed_quad()`, `linear_predictor_value()`, `chain_rule_d1()` to `chain_rule_d4()`, and the member helpers of `row_model_view` and `outer_pass_layout`) can be called on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device. Design helpers, `build_row_model()`, `assemble_row_model()`, `assemble_outer_row_model()`, `penalty_evaluator`, `make_penalty_term()`, `for_each_penalty_entry()`, `penalty_set`, `row_model_objective`, `row_model_composite_objective`, `fit_inner()`, `laplace_marginal_criterion`, and `fit_marginal()` run on the host, with `assemble_row_model()` and `assemble_outer_row_model()` launching the `assemble_rows()` and `assemble_outer_rows()` kernels on `Kokkos::DefaultExecutionSpace`. A program that includes `inner_fit.hpp` or `marginal_criterion.hpp` links `Laplace::dense` and `Laplace::sparse`, since those headers hold the curvature and sparse \f$L D L^\top\f$ solvers. On four simulated models and two models with frozen reference fits of 2000 units, on gcc and CUDA, row kernels match dense reference evaluations within \f$10^{-12}\f$ of scale, smooth inner fits match frozen reference solutions within \f$10^{-9}\f$, proximal Newton fits with kinked penalties satisfy optimality conditions within \f$2.5 \times 10^{-8}\f$ of \f$1/s\f$ (lasso) and \f$7.7 \times 10^{-6}\f$ of \f$1/(2\sigma)\f$ (group lasso), exact marginal gradients and Hessians in nine simulated cases match Richardson-extrapolated central differences with errors at most \f$2.0 \times 10^{-10}\f$ and \f$6.1 \times 10^{-11}\f$ on gcc (at most \f$5.7 \times 10^{-10}\f$ and \f$1.2 \times 10^{-10}\f$ on CUDA), and marginal fits reproduce the frozen reference optima within \f$6 \times 10^{-8}\f$.

## Model specification and design rows

A model operates on \f$n\f$ observation units, \f$1 \le R \le 4\f$ linear predictors per unit (`row_predictor_capacity = 4`), and a distribution with \f$1 \le P \le 6\f$ natural parameters (`row_param_capacity = 6`). Each unit \f$i \in \{0, \dots, n - 1\}\f$ has linear predictors
\f[
\eta_r(i) = o_r(i) + \sum_{e=0}^{W - 1} v_{ie}\, x_{c(i, e)}, \qquad r = 0, \dots, R - 1,
\f]
where \f$o_r(i)\f$ is an optional offset (zero if `offset` or `offset[r]` is empty), \f$v_{ie}\f$ is entry \f$e\f$ of the stored design row of unit \f$i\f$, \f$c(i, e)\f$ is its index in the vector of coordinates \f$x \in \mathbb{R}^N\f$, and entries with `coefficient == -1` are empty. All blocks of \ref laplace::engine::design_entries "design_entries" are stacked to a uniform row width \f$W\f$, the sum of the individual block widths, so that device threads executing across units do not diverge.

Four host helpers build `design_entries` blocks:
- \ref laplace::engine::design_column "design_column()" creates a width-1 block mapping a covariate vector to a single coordinate.
- \ref laplace::engine::design_basis "design_basis()" evaluates any basis satisfying \ref basis at a covariate vector, filling `width = basis.row_width()` entries per unit starting at `first_coefficient` and setting indices listed in `dropped` to `-1` for identifiability.
- \ref laplace::engine::design_group_effect "design_group_effect()" creates a width-1 block in which unit \f$i\f$ reads coordinate \f$j\f$ of its own group in the first grouping.
- \ref laplace::engine::design_crossed_effect "design_crossed_effect()" creates a width-1 block in which unit \f$i\f$ reads coordinate \f$j\f$ of its own group in crossed grouping \f$k \ge 1\f$ (`row_model_spec::crossed[k - 1]`).

A model supports up to `row_grouping_capacity = 4` groupings (\f$0 \le m \le 4\f$): an optional first grouping given by `group`, `n_groups`, and `group_width`, and further groupings crossed with it in `row_model_spec::crossed`, each a \ref laplace::engine::row_grouping "row_grouping" holding `group`, `n_groups`, and `width` (a crossed grouping requires a first grouping). Coordinates \f$x\f$ are partitioned into \f$n_{\mathrm{global}}\f$ global coordinates shared by all units, followed by the blocks of grouping \f$0\f$ and then each crossed grouping \f$k = 1, \dots, m - 1\f$, where grouping \f$k\f$ has \f$G_k\f$ groups of width \f$d_k\f$ starting at \ref laplace::engine::grouping_first_coordinate "grouping_first_coordinate(spec, k)" (\f$c_k\f$), giving
\f[
N = n_{\mathrm{global}} + \sum_{k=0}^{m-1} G_k d_k
\f]
total coordinates. In grouping \f$k\f$, group \f$\gamma \in \{0, \dots, G_k - 1\}\f$ occupies indices \f$c_k + \gamma d_k, \dots, c_k + (\gamma + 1) d_k - 1\f$. Each unit may reference only global coordinates and, in each grouping, the coordinates of its own group.

## Pointwise nodes, supports, and packed tensors

Per-unit model values are numbered sequentially: indices \f$0, \dots, R - 1\f$ hold the linear predictors \f$\eta_0, \dots, \eta_{R-1}\f$, and index \f$R + k\f$ holds the output of node \f$k\f$ (\f$0 \le k < n_{\mathrm{nodes}} \le 8\f$, `row_node_capacity = 8`, `row_value_capacity = 12`). Nodes must be listed in topological order so that every input of node \f$k\f$ has index strictly less than \f$R + k\f$. The array `row_model_spec::param_value` maps each of the \f$P\f$ distribution parameters to one value index.

Two node kinds (`pointwise_node_kind`) are supported:
- `link`: takes one input \f$u\f$ and evaluates \f$\varphi^{-1}(u)\f$, where \f$\varphi\f$ is `pointwise_node::link`, a `bijector::scalar_bijector` (\ref bijector_scalar "scalar bijectors").
- `logistic_curve`: takes three inputs \f$(A, x_m, s)\f$ and reads a data column \f$t\f$ (`data_column`) to evaluate \f$f(A, x_m, s; t) = A / (1 + e^z)\f$, where \f$z = (x_m - t)/s\f$. Setting \f$p = 1 / (1 + e^z)\f$, \f$q = p(1 - p)\f$, and \f$w = 1 - 2p\f$, the partial derivatives in \f$(A, x_m, s)\f$ (verified symbolically with SymPy; Meurer and others, 2017) are:
\f[
\begin{aligned}
f_A &= p, &
f_{x_m} &= -\frac{A q}{s}, &
f_s &= \frac{A q z}{s}, \\
f_{AA} &= 0, &
f_{A, x_m} &= -\frac{q}{s}, &
f_{A, s} &= \frac{q z}{s}, \\
f_{x_m, x_m} &= \frac{A q w}{s^2}, &
f_{x_m, s} &= \frac{A q (1 - w z)}{s^2}, &
f_{s, s} &= \frac{A q z (w z - 2)}{s^2}.
\end{aligned}
\f]
The third- and fourth-order helpers \ref laplace::engine::logistic_curve_d3 "logistic_curve_d3()" and \ref laplace::engine::logistic_curve_d4 "logistic_curve_d4()" evaluate the packed partial derivatives via \f$f = A\, h(x_m, s)\f$ with \f$h = g(z)\f$, \f$g(z) = 1 / (1 + e^z)\f$, and \f$g_1 = -q\f$, \f$g_2 = w q\f$, \f$g_3 = 2 q^2 - w^2 q\f$, \f$g_4 = w^3 q - 8 w q^2\f$ (all 34 forms derived and verified with SymPy in `tools/reference/engine_nodes.py`).

Each value is carried in a \ref laplace::engine::predictor_value "predictor_value\<R, K\>" (default \f$K = 2\f$, or \f$3, 4\f$) holding the scalar `v`, a bit mask `support` whose bit \f$r\f$ is set when the value depends on \f$\eta_r\f$, first derivatives `d1[R]`, and second derivatives `d2` packed on sorted pairs \f$0 \le a \le b < R\f$ in lexicographic order via \ref laplace::engine::packed_pair "packed_pair(a, b, R)" \f$= a R - a(a - 1)/2 + (b - a)\f$. When \f$K \ge 3\f$, `d3` stores third derivatives packed on sorted triples \f$0 \le a \le b \le c < R\f$ via \ref laplace::engine::packed_triple "packed_triple\<R\>(a, b, c)", and when \f$K \ge 4\f$, `d4` stores fourth derivatives packed on sorted quadruples \f$0 \le a \le b \le c \le d < R\f$ via \ref laplace::engine::packed_quad "packed_quad\<R\>(a, b, c, d)", both matching `distrib::sym_tensor_position` (\ref distrib_core "distribution core").

For a node \f$o = f(i_0, \dots, i_{M-1})\f$ with \f$M \in \{1, 3\}\f$ inputs (`pointwise_node_input_capacity = 3`), \ref laplace::engine::chain_rule_d1 "chain_rule_d1()" sets `o.support` to the bitwise union of input supports and evaluates only entries in `o.support`, while \ref laplace::engine::chain_rule_d2 "chain_rule_d2()" assumes `o.support` is already set and evaluates only the second-order entries with both indices in `o.support`:
\f[
\begin{aligned}
o_a &= \sum_{p=0}^{M-1} f_p\, i_{p, a}, \\
o_{ab} &= \sum_{p=0}^{M-1} f_p\, i_{p, ab} + \sum_{p=0}^{M-1} \sum_{q=0}^{M-1} f_{pq}\, i_{p, a}\, i_{q, b},
\end{aligned}
\f]
where \f$f_{pq}\f$ are packed on sorted input pairs. Splitting the first and second orders ensures that kernels requiring only order-1 derivatives never compute second-order node derivatives.

For higher-order passes, \ref laplace::engine::chain_rule_d3 "chain_rule_d3()" (\f$K \ge 3\f$) and \ref laplace::engine::chain_rule_d4 "chain_rule_d4()" (\f$K \ge 4\f$) also assume `o.support` is set, write only their own order, and include a term only when every block of predictor indices passed to an input lies in that input's support. At order three, with \f$f_{pqr}\f$ packed on sorted input triples,
\f[
o_{abc} = \sum_{p=0}^{M-1} f_p\, i_{p, abc} + \sum_{p=0}^{M-1}\sum_{q=0}^{M-1} f_{pq}\,(i_{p, ab}\, i_{q, c} + i_{p, ac}\, i_{q, b} + i_{p, bc}\, i_{q, a}) + \sum_{p=0}^{M-1}\sum_{q=0}^{M-1}\sum_{r=0}^{M-1} f_{pqr}\, i_{p, a}\, i_{q, b}\, i_{r, c}.
\f]
At order four, `chain_rule_d4()` evaluates one term for each partition of the four indices \f$\{a, b, c, d\}\f$ into blocks, summed over repeated inputs within their supports.

## Row kernels and blocking

\ref laplace::engine::build_row_model "build_row_model(spec, P, rows_per_block)" validates the specification, stably sorts units by their tuple of groups across all \f$m\f$ groupings (preserving the original index mapping in `row_model::order`), partitions each tuple into contiguous blocks of at most `rows_per_block` units (default \f$16\f$), remaps every design entry coordinate to a local slot \f$s \in \{0, \dots, L - 1\}\f$ with \f$L = n_{\mathrm{global}} + \sum_{k=0}^{m-1} d_k \le 32\f$ (`row_local_capacity = 32`, or `-1` for empty entries), computes the information coordinate maps (`row_model::information_row`, `row_model::information_column`), and copies the arrays to `row_model_view` in `Kokkos::DefaultExecutionSpace::memory_space`.

On the host, \ref laplace::engine::assemble_row_model "assemble_row_model\<List\>(m, x, q, out)" switches on `m.distrib` within `distrib_list<Ds...>` and on \f$R \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$, launches `assemble_rows<D, R, Q>()` over the blocks using `Kokkos::Experimental::ScatterView`, and copies the result into `row_assembly`. Inside each kernel, the per-unit node walk evaluates nodes in topological order up to the derivative order required by `q` and no higher. Each block accumulates its local value, local gradient, and local lower-triangular Hessian in thread-local buffers of size at most \f$L\f$ and \f$L(L+1)/2\f$ and contributes its sums once.

Let \f$\ell_i = \log f(y_i; \theta_i)\f$ denote the log-density (or log-mass) of unit \f$i\f$ at parameter vector \f$\theta_i = (\theta_{i,0}, \dots, \theta_{i,P-1})\f$ and \f$w_i\f$ its weight (`1.0` when `weight` is empty). The four values of \ref laplace::engine::row_quantity "row_quantity" compute:
- `value`: node order \f$0\f$, returning \f$f(x) = -\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} w_i \ell_i\f$.
- `gradient`: node order \f$1\f$, returning \f$f(x)\f$ and \f$\nabla f(x)\f$.
- `observed_information`: node order \f$2\f$, returning \f$f(x)\f$, \f$\nabla f(x)\f$, and the observed Hessian \f$\nabla^2 f(x)\f$.
- `expected_information`: node order \f$1\f$, returning \f$f(x)\f$, \f$\nabla f(x)\f$, and \f$\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} w_i J_i^\top I(\theta_i) J_i\f$, where \f$J_i = \mathrm{d}\theta_i / \mathrm{d}x\f$ and \f$I(\theta_i)\f$ is the distribution's expected information matrix.

With \f$\theta = \theta(\eta)\f$, the chain rule from parameters \f$\theta\f$ to linear predictors \f$\eta\f$ gives
\f[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \eta_a} &= \sum_{j=0}^{P-1} \ell_j\, \theta_{j, a}, \\
\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \eta_a \partial \eta_b} &= \sum_{j=0}^{P-1} \ell_j\, \theta_{j, ab} + \sum_{j=0}^{P-1} \sum_{k=0}^{P-1} \ell_{jk}\, \theta_{j, a}\, \theta_{k, b}, \\
\mathbb{E}\!\left[-\frac{\partial^2 \ell}{\partial \eta_a \partial \eta_b}\right] &= \sum_{j=0}^{P-1} \sum_{k=0}^{P-1} I_{jk}\, \theta_{j, a}\, \theta_{k, b},
\end{aligned}
\f]
where \f$\ell_j = \partial \ell / \partial \theta_j\f$, \f$\ell_{jk} = \partial^2 \ell / \partial \theta_j \partial \theta_k\f$, and \f$I_{jk} = \mathbb{E}[-\ell_{jk}]\f$. In the expected information, the second derivatives \f$\theta_{j, ab}\f$ of the nodes multiply the score \f$\ell_j\f$, whose expectation under the model is zero; those terms drop out, so `expected_information` requires the nodes to order one only. Because \f$\eta_r(i)\f$ is affine in \f$x\f$, derivatives in \f$x\f$ follow by multiplying those in \f$\eta\f$ by the stored design entries \f$v_{ie}\f$.

## Information layout and sparse curvature

Because units read only global coordinates and, in each grouping, the coordinates of their own group, the assembled information matrix has a structured lower-triangular layout of size `row_model_view::information_size`, indexed on the device by \ref laplace::engine::row_model_view::information_position "row_model_view::information_position(b, r, c)" and paired on the host with coordinate arrays `row_model::information_row` and `row_model::information_column` (`row` \f$\ge\f$ `column`):
1. The \f$n_{\mathrm{global}} \times n_{\mathrm{global}}\f$ global block, lower triangle stored by rows.
2. For each grouping \f$k = 0, \dots, m - 1\f$ (starting at `grouping_information[k]`), group \f$\gamma = 0, \dots, G_k - 1\f$ after group: its \f$d_k \times d_k\f$ square block lower triangle by rows, followed by its \f$d_k \times n_{\mathrm{global}}\f$ cross block with the globals by rows. With a single grouping, parts 1 and 2 form an arrowhead matrix.
3. For each pair of groupings \f$(l, k)\f$ with \f$0 \le l < k < m\f$ (at `pair_index(l, k)`, starting at `pair_information[p]`): one \f$d_k \times d_l\f$ block stored by rows (the coordinate of \f$k\f$ as row and that of \f$l\f$ as column) for each pair of groups \f$(\gamma_l, \gamma_k)\f$ met by at least one unit (`block_pairs`).

When `row_model_objective` is instantiated with `optimizers::sparse_curvature_matrix`, it builds the compressed-sparse-column lower-triangle pattern once at construction by joining the positions of this information layout with the diagonal and with every pair of coordinates \f$(a, b)\f$ (\f$a > b\f$) belonging to a replicate of any penalty term (so replicates spanning groups or mixing globals and groups contribute their entries outside the layout), rows sorted in each column with the diagonal entry first. It passes the pattern to `optimizers::sparse_curvature_solver`, which factorizes it via \ref sparse "sparse_ldlt". Unlike `optimizers::dense_curvature_matrix`, sparse curvature has no eigendecomposition route and no negative-curvature search in `optimizers::newton_optimizer` (\ref optimizers_second_order "Newton and trust region"): if an assembled sparse matrix is not positive definite, Newton falls back to steepest descent.

## Inner objective, penalties, caching, and fitting

A penalty of the engine is any penalty satisfying \ref penalty "laplace::penalty": \ref penalty_distrib_penalty "distrib_penalty\<M\>" (for a multivariate distribution or a product of univariate distributions), \ref penalty_linear_map "linear_map_penalty", or the separable \ref penalty_nonsmooth "SCAD and MCP penalties". It represents minus the logarithm of a prior with its normalizing constant included, proper or intrinsic (flat along a null space), or an unnormalizable penalty. The covariance and precision forms of a Gaussian prior are two separate distributions (\ref distrib_mvgaussian1 "mvgaussian1_distrib" and \ref distrib_mvgaussian2 "mvgaussian2_distrib"), not an option of the engine.

A \ref laplace::engine::penalty_term "penalty_term" (built by \ref laplace::engine::make_penalty_term "make_penalty_term()") wraps a type-erased host \ref laplace::engine::penalty_evaluator "penalty_evaluator" (implemented by \ref laplace::engine::penalty_evaluator_of "penalty_evaluator_of\<P\>") and applies one penalty to one or more `replicates`, each a gathered list of `dim()` distinct model coordinates in any order; the replicates share the hyperparameters so the parameter state is computed once. Each hyperparameter \f$\theta_j\f$ of the penalty is either fixed at a natural value (`theta_source[j] == -1`, reading `theta_value[j]`) or reads an outer coordinate \f$a = \f$ `theta_source[j]` \f$\ge 0\f$: \f$\theta_j = \phi_a(u_a)\f$, where \f$\phi_a\f$ is the inverse of the \ref bijector_scalar "scalar_bijector" of \ref laplace::engine::penalty_hyperparameter "penalty_hyperparameter". Two penalties share a hyperparameter by reading the same outer coordinate. Derivatives of a penalty evaluator up to `penalty_max_beta_order = 4` in \f$\beta\f$ and `penalty_max_theta_order = 2` in \f$\theta\f$ follow the block layout of the penalty and are traversed on the host by \ref laplace::engine::for_each_penalty_entry "for_each_penalty_entry()"; \ref laplace::engine::penalty_set "penalty_set" manages the cloned evaluators and their current natural hyperparameters on the host.

\ref laplace::engine::row_model_objective "row_model_objective\<List, Curvature\>" satisfies `optimizers::curvature_objective` and minimizes
\f[
F(x) = -\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} w_i \ell_i(x) + \sum_{t} \sum_{r} \mathrm{pen}_t(x_{C_{t,r}}; \theta_t),
\f]
where \f$C_{t,r}\f$ is replicate \f$r\f$ of penalty term \f$t\f$ and \f$\theta_t\f$ are its current natural hyperparameters. Away from a kink, a penalty with kinks evaluates every derivative; at a kink, its derivatives are one-sided. The curvature option \ref laplace::engine::row_curvature "row_curvature" selects `observed` (observed Hessian, default for `dense_curvature_matrix`), `expected` (expected information plus penalties), or `observed_else_expected` (default for `sparse_curvature_matrix`: tries the observed Hessian and, if `factorize()` is not `positive_definite`, re-assembles `expected_information` and increments `expected_fallbacks`). Held coordinate indices passed in `held` keep their values: their gradient entries are set to \f$0\f$ and their rows and columns in \f$H\f$ become those of the identity (\f$1\f$ on the diagonal, \f$0\f$ off-diagonal).

The objective caches the last evaluation point and assembly (`last_assembly()`, excluding penalties). A call at the exact same point `x` requiring no higher kernel level than already cached increments `counts().reused` and launches no kernel. \ref laplace::engine::row_model_objective::set_hyperparameters "set_hyperparameters()" updates the natural outer hyperparameters without invalidating the cached assembly, and \ref laplace::engine::row_model_objective::observed_value_gradient_curvature "observed_value_gradient_curvature()" always assembles the observed Hessian regardless of `row_curvature`. \ref laplace::engine::row_kernel_counts "row_kernel_counts" records kernel calls per `row_quantity`, total assembly wall time in `seconds`, `expected_fallbacks`, and `reused`. \ref laplace::engine::fit_inner "fit_inner(objective, x0, newton)" resets the objective's counts, runs `optimizers::minimize()`, and returns an \ref laplace::engine::inner_fit_report "inner_fit_report".

For models containing penalties with kinks, \ref laplace::engine::row_model_composite_objective "row_model_composite_objective\<List\>" implements the composite objective interface for `optimizers::proximal_newton_optimizer` (\ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods"). It splits the penalty terms by `penalty_kink_geometry`: smooth penalties stay with the log-likelihood in a dense `row_model_objective` (observed curvature by default, since proximal Newton corrects indefinite Hessians), while kinked penalties form the non-smooth part evaluated through `nonsmooth_value()`, `nonsmooth_prox()`, and per-block proximal operators (`penalty_set`). Hyperparameters of kinked penalties are fixed. Each coordinate of a separable kinked penalty (such as a lasso via univariate Laplace priors, SCAD, or MCP) forms one block (`nonsmooth_block_separable(b) == true`, range length \f$1\f$), so the optimizer runs coordinate descent on its subproblem; any non-separable kinked penalty (such as a group lasso) forms one block per replicate (`nonsmooth_block_range(b)` reports length \f$0\f$), and the optimizer runs FISTA on the subproblem. Construction throws `std::invalid_argument` if a kinked penalty has no proximal operator, if two replicates with kinks share a coordinate, or if a kinked penalty reads a held coordinate.

## Marginal criterion

\ref laplace::engine::laplace_marginal_criterion "laplace_marginal_criterion\<List\>" (in `<laplace/engine/marginal_criterion.hpp>`) exposes the Laplace approximation of the marginal likelihood of a smooth model, together with its exact gradient and exact dense Hessian, as a `curvature_objective` for the library's optimizers. Its inner objective uses `row_model_objective` with `optimizers::sparse_curvature_matrix` and minimizes
\f[
F(x; u) = -\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} w_i \ell_i(x) + \sum_{t} \sum_{r} \mathrm{pen}_t\bigl(x_{C_{t,r}}; \theta_t(u)\bigr),
\f]
where each penalty is minus the logarithm of a prior with its normalizing constant included (proper, or intrinsic with the pseudo-determinant over its range).

The outer parameter vector is \f$u = (u_0, \dots, u_{n_{\mathrm{hyp}} - 1}, \psi)\f$ of length \f$n_u = n_{\mathrm{hyp}} + |\psi|\f$, where \f$u_0, \dots, u_{n_{\mathrm{hyp}} - 1}\f$ are the \f$n_{\mathrm{hyp}}\f$ free outer hyperparameter coordinates (`hyperparameters`, whose natural values \f$\phi_a(u_a)\f$ are returned by \ref laplace::engine::laplace_marginal_criterion::natural_hyperparameters "natural_hyperparameters()") and \f$\psi\f$ are coordinates of \f$x\f$ (`outer_coordinates`) held in the inner fit and estimated on the criterion. Any coordinates passed in `fixed` stay fixed at their initial values in `x0` and are not estimated; the remaining \f$q\f$ coordinates are free and integrated by the Laplace approximation. With \f$\hat x\f$ the inner minimum at \f$u\f$ and \f$H\f$ the observed Hessian of \f$F\f$ in the \f$q\f$ free coordinates at \f$\hat x\f$ (factorized by `sparse::sparse_ldlt`, whose `log_abs_determinant()` gives \f$\log|H|\f$), the criterion minimized is
\f[
V(u) = F(\hat x; u) + \frac{1}{2} \log|H| - \frac{q}{2} \log 2\pi,
\f]
which is minus the logarithm of the Laplace approximation of the marginal likelihood, with no constant left out (an intrinsic penalty is flat along its null space, so those directions are integrated against Lebesgue measure).

The constructor throws `std::invalid_argument` if `x0` does not have length \f$N\f$, if any penalty is unnormalizable (`penalty_normalization::unnormalizable`, such as SCAD or MCP), if any penalty has kinks (`penalty_kink_geometry::none` is required), if any hyperparameter index in \f$0, \dots, n_{\mathrm{hyp}} - 1\f$ is unused, if the outer vector is empty (`n_outer() < 1`), or if `penalty_set` or `row_model_objective` validation fails. Evaluating the Hessian (`value_gradient_curvature()`) also throws `std::invalid_argument` if `n_outer() > row_outer_direction_capacity` or if any estimated hyperparameter belongs to a penalty that is not \f$C^2\f$ in \f$\theta\f$ (`theta_regularity() < 2`).

Each evaluation at a new \f$u\f$ maps \f$u_a\f$ to natural hyperparameters (returning \f$V(u) = +\infty\f$ if any natural value is non-finite), updates the inner objective, and starts `fit_inner()` from the previous inner minimum \f$\hat x\f$ (with \f$\psi\f$ set from \f$u\f$). If an inner fit stops with `optimizer_status::stalled` because the step fell below rounding before the decrement reached tolerance, its point is used and `inner_stalls` is incremented; if it fails otherwise, \f$V(u) = +\infty\f$, `inner_failures` is incremented, and `last_inner_failure` records the status. After `fit_inner()`, the criterion always assembles the observed Hessian via `observed_value_gradient_curvature()` with no fallback to expected information, and factorizes \f$H\f$ with `sparse_ldlt::factorize()`. When polishing is enabled (the default; controlled by \ref laplace::engine::laplace_marginal_criterion::set_polishing "set_polishing()"), one Newton step \f$d = -H^{-1} g\f$ is taken on the free coordinates using that factor, `polishing_steps` is incremented, and the observed Hessian and objective are assembled and factorized again at the polished \f$\hat x\f$. Because the inner residual \f$d\f$ enters \f$\frac{1}{2}\log|H|\f$ at first order through \f$\tau^\top d\f$, polishing reduces the residual to \f$\|d\|^2\f$ at the cost of one extra order-2 assembly and one sparse factorization per evaluation. If `sparse_ldlt::factorize()` does not return `positive_definite`, \f$V(u) = +\infty\f$ and `not_positive_definite` is incremented. Whenever \f$V(u) = +\infty\f$, `value_gradient()` and `value_gradient_curvature()` fill the output gradient and Hessian with quiet NaN. Repeated calls at the same \f$u\f$ reuse cached values and derivatives.

The exact gradient and Hessian of \f$V(u)\f$ read two device passes over the units in `<laplace/engine/outer_rows.hpp>`, launched by \ref laplace::engine::assemble_outer_row_model "assemble_outer_row_model\<List\>()" through \ref laplace::engine::assemble_outer_rows "assemble_outer_rows()". Write \f$T_{\mathrm{lik}}\f$ and \f$Q_{\mathrm{lik}}\f$ for the third and fourth derivatives in \f$x\f$ of the unpenalized term \f$-\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} w_i \ell_i(x)\f$, \f$T = T_{\mathrm{lik}} + T_{\mathrm{pen}}\f$ and \f$Q = Q_{\mathrm{lik}} + Q_{\mathrm{pen}}\f$ for the total third and fourth derivatives of \f$F\f$ in \f$x\f$ (with \f$T_{\mathrm{pen}}\f$ and \f$Q_{\mathrm{pen}}\f$ evaluated on the host), \f$H_I\f$ for the symmetric \f$N \times N\f$ inverse of \f$H\f$ padded with zeros on held rows and columns, and \f$v_0, \dots, v_{n_u - 1}\f$ for the outer sensitivity directions in \f$x\f$. Each kernel evaluates nodes only up to its own order and contracts each unit's design row once with the third and fourth predictor derivatives of \f$-\ell_i\f$:
- Order 3 (`order == 3`, used for \f$\nabla V\f$): computes the \f$N\f$-vector \f$\tau_{\mathrm{lik}, c} = \sum_{r, s=0}^{N-1} (H_I)_{rs}\, (T_{\mathrm{lik}})_{rsc}\f$.
- Order 4 (`order == 4`, used for \f$\nabla^2 V\f$): computes \f$\tau_{\mathrm{lik}}\f$ and, for each direction \f$a \in \{0, \dots, n_u - 1\}\f$ and sorted pair \f$0 \le a \le b < n_u\f$, the matrix \f$T_{\mathrm{lik}}[v_a]_{rs} = \sum_{c=0}^{N-1} (T_{\mathrm{lik}})_{rsc}\, v_{a, c}\f$ in the information layout, the vector \f$T_{\mathrm{lik}}[v_a, v_b]_c = \sum_{r, s=0}^{N-1} (T_{\mathrm{lik}})_{crs}\, v_{a, r}\, v_{b, s}\f$, and the scalar \f$q_{\mathrm{lik}, ab} = \sum_{r, s, t, w=0}^{N-1} (H_I)_{rs}\, (Q_{\mathrm{lik}})_{rstw}\, v_{a, t}\, v_{b, w}\f$.

The pass output is laid out in one vector by \ref laplace::engine::outer_pass_layout "outer_pass_layout". Because block accumulators reside on the thread stack, `assemble_outer_row_model()` throws `std::invalid_argument` if a block has more than `row_outer_local_capacity = 16` local slots or if the order-4 pass receives more than `row_outer_direction_capacity = 4` directions (`value_gradient_curvature()` enforces the same limit on `n_outer()`).

For the penalty contributions, write \f$P_a = \partial F / \partial u_a\f$ and \f$P_{ab} = \partial^2 F / \partial u_a \partial u_b\f$ at fixed \f$x\f$ (both zero for \f$\psi\f$). Through the bijectors \f$\theta_j = \phi_a(u_a)\f$, summed over all penalty terms and their replicates,
\f[
\begin{aligned}
P_a &= \sum_{j:\, \mathrm{src}(j) = a} \phi_a'(u_a) \frac{\partial\,\mathrm{pen}}{\partial \theta_j}, \\
P_{ab} &= \sum_{\substack{j:\, \mathrm{src}(j) = a \\ k:\, \mathrm{src}(k) = b}} \phi_a'(u_a)\,\phi_b'(u_b) \frac{\partial^2\,\mathrm{pen}}{\partial \theta_j \partial \theta_k} + \delta_{ab}\,\phi_a''(u_a) \sum_{j:\, \mathrm{src}(j) = a} \frac{\partial\,\mathrm{pen}}{\partial \theta_j},
\end{aligned}
\f]
where \f$\mathrm{src}(j)\f$ is `theta_source[j]` and \f$\delta_{ab}\f$ is the Kronecker delta. Their spatial derivatives are \f$s_a = \nabla_x P_a\f$, \f$s_{ab} = \nabla_x P_{ab}\f$, \f$E_a = \nabla_x^2 P_a\f$, \f$E_{ab} = \nabla_x^2 P_{ab}\f$, and \f$\tau^{(a)}_c = \sum_{r, s=0}^{N-1} (H_I)_{rs}\, \partial^3 P_a / \partial x_r \partial x_s \partial x_c\f$ (all zero for \f$\psi\f$). Let \f$H_{\mathrm{full}}\f$ be the observed Hessian of \f$F\f$ in all \f$N\f$ coordinates, \f$g_{\mathrm{full}} = \nabla_x F(\hat x; u)\f$, and \f$\tau_c = \sum_{r, s=0}^{N-1} (H_I)_{rs}\, T_{rsc}\f$. With sensitivity \f$v_a = \mathrm{d}\hat x / \mathrm{d}u_a\f$ given by \f$v_a = -H_I s_a\f$ for a hyperparameter \f$a < n_{\mathrm{hyp}}\f$ and \f$v_a = e_c - H_I H_{\mathrm{full}} e_c\f$ for an outer coordinate \f$\psi_{a - n_{\mathrm{hyp}}}\f$ at index \f$c\f$ (\f$e_c\f$ the \f$c\f$-th coordinate unit vector), and with \f$\dot H_a = E_a + T[v_a]\f$, the gradient and Hessian of \f$V(u)\f$ are
\f[
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla V)_a &= P_a + [a \ge n_{\mathrm{hyp}}]\, g_{\mathrm{full}}^\top e_c + \frac{1}{2}\bigl(\operatorname{tr}(H_I E_a) + \tau^\top v_a\bigr), \\
(\nabla^2 V)_{ab} &= F_{2, ab} + \frac{1}{2}\Bigl(-\operatorname{tr}(H_I \dot H_a H_I \dot H_b) + \operatorname{tr}(H_I E_{ab}) + {\tau^{(a)}}^\top v_b + {\tau^{(b)}}^\top v_a + Q[H_I; v_a, v_b] + \tau^\top \hat x_{ab}\Bigr),
\end{aligned}
\f]
where \f$[a \ge n_{\mathrm{hyp}}]\f$ is \f$1\f$ for \f$\psi\f$ and \f$0\f$ otherwise, \f$Q[H_I; v_a, v_b] = \sum_{r, s, t, w=0}^{N-1} (H_I)_{rs}\, Q_{rstw}\, v_{a, t}\, v_{b, w}\f$, and
\f[
\begin{aligned}
F_{2, ab} &= P_{ab} + s_a^\top v_b + s_b^\top v_a + v_a^\top H_{\mathrm{full}} v_b, \\
\hat x_{ab} &= -H_I \bigl(T[v_a, v_b] + E_b v_a + E_a v_b + s_{ab}\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\f]

Forming \f$H_I\f$ as a dense \f$N \times N\f$ matrix costs \f$N^2\f$ doubles of memory on host and device and up to \f$N\f$ sparse triangular solves from the \f$L D L^\top\f$ factorization per new \f$u\f$. Computing \f$\operatorname{tr}(H_I \dot H_a H_I \dot H_b)\f$ forms the dense \f$N \times N \times n_u\f$ device array \f$X_a = H_I \dot H_a\f$ (\f$n_u N^2\f$ doubles and \f$O(N\,\mathrm{nnz}(\dot H_a))\f$ work per direction), followed by \f$n_u(n_u + 1)/2\f$ device trace reductions. \ref laplace::engine::fit_marginal "fit_marginal(criterion, u0, newton)" runs `optimizers::minimize()` with `optimizers::newton_optimizer` on the dense \f$n_u \times n_u\f$ Hessian and returns a \ref laplace::engine::marginal_fit_report "marginal_fit_report" containing the optimizer report, \ref laplace::engine::marginal_criterion_counts "marginal_criterion_counts" (including `polishing_steps` and `seconds_penalties`), and the final `inner_point`.

## Example

The host function below builds a Gaussian model with \f$\mu_i = \beta_0 + \beta_1 x_i\f$ and \f$\log \sigma_i = \gamma_0\f$ using a `link` node and fits it with `fit_inner()`:

```cpp
#include <laplace/bijector/scalar.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/gaussian1.hpp>
#include <laplace/engine/inner_fit.hpp>

#include <vector>

laplace::engine::inner_fit_report fit_gaussian_log_scale(
    const std::vector<double>& x_cov,
    const std::vector<double>& y) {
  const int n = static_cast<int>(y.size());
  const std::vector<double> ones(n, 1.0);

  laplace::engine::row_model_spec spec;
  spec.n_predictors = 2;
  spec.n_global = 3;  // coordinates: 0 = beta0, 1 = beta1, 2 = gamma0
  spec.y = y;
  spec.design = {
      laplace::engine::design_column(0, ones, 0),
      laplace::engine::design_column(0, x_cov, 1),
      laplace::engine::design_column(1, ones, 2),
  };

  laplace::engine::pointwise_node sigma_node;
  sigma_node.kind = laplace::engine::pointwise_node_kind::link;
  sigma_node.input[0] = 1;  // linear predictor 1 -> value 2
  sigma_node.link = laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector{
      laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector_map::log};
  spec.nodes = {sigma_node};
  spec.param_value = {0, 2};  // mu = value 0, sigma = value 2
  spec.distrib = 0;

  using list = laplace::engine::distrib_list<laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib>;
  const laplace::engine::row_model model =
      laplace::engine::build_row_model(spec, laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib::n_params);
  laplace::engine::row_model_objective<list> obj(model, {});

  laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector x0("x0", model.n_coordinates());
  x0(0) = 0.0;
  x0(1) = 0.0;
  x0(2) = 0.0;
  return laplace::engine::fit_inner(obj, x0);
}
```

## Marginal fit example

The host function below extends the Gaussian model of the example above with a random group intercept \f$\alpha_g \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\alpha^2)\f$, \f$\sigma_\alpha = e^{u_0}\f$, using a `distrib_penalty` over `univariate_as_multivariate<gaussian1_distrib>` reading outer coordinate `0` through `scalar_bijector::lower_bounded(0.0)`, and minimizes \f$V(u_0)\f$ with `fit_marginal()`:

```cpp
#include <laplace/bijector/scalar.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/gaussian1.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/univariate_as_multivariate.hpp>
#include <laplace/engine/marginal_criterion.hpp>
#include <laplace/penalty/distrib_penalty.hpp>
#include <laplace/penalty/independent_product_penalty.hpp>

#include <vector>

laplace::engine::marginal_fit_report fit_gaussian_marginal(
    const std::vector<double>& x_cov,
    const std::vector<int>& group,
    int n_groups,
    const std::vector<double>& y) {
  const int n = static_cast<int>(y.size());
  const std::vector<double> ones(n, 1.0);

  laplace::engine::row_model_spec spec;
  spec.n_predictors = 2;
  spec.n_global = 3;  // coordinates: 0 = beta0, 1 = beta1, 2 = gamma0, 3.. = alpha_g
  spec.n_groups = n_groups;
  spec.group_width = 1;
  spec.group = group;
  spec.y = y;
  spec.design = {
      laplace::engine::design_column(0, ones, 0),
      laplace::engine::design_column(0, x_cov, 1),
      laplace::engine::design_column(1, ones, 2),
      laplace::engine::design_group_effect(spec, 0, ones, 0),
  };

  laplace::engine::pointwise_node sigma_node;
  sigma_node.kind = laplace::engine::pointwise_node_kind::link;
  sigma_node.input[0] = 1;
  sigma_node.link = laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector{
      laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector_map::log};
  spec.nodes = {sigma_node};
  spec.param_value = {0, 2};
  spec.distrib = 0;

  using list = laplace::engine::distrib_list<laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib>;
  const laplace::engine::row_model model =
      laplace::engine::build_row_model(spec, laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib::n_params);

  using prior_law = laplace::distrib::univariate_as_multivariate<laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib>;
  const laplace::penalty::distrib_penalty<prior_law> prior{prior_law(laplace::distrib::gaussian1_distrib{})};
  std::vector<std::vector<int>> reps;
  reps.reserve(n_groups);
  for (int g = 0; g < n_groups; ++g) reps.push_back({spec.n_global + g});
  const laplace::engine::penalty_term group_prior =
      laplace::engine::make_penalty_term(prior, std::move(reps), {0});

  const std::vector<laplace::engine::penalty_hyperparameter> hyper = {
      {laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::lower_bounded(0.0)},
  };
  std::vector<double> x0(model.n_coordinates(), 0.0);
  laplace::engine::laplace_marginal_criterion<list> criterion(
      model, {group_prior}, hyper, {}, {}, x0);

  laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector u0("u0", criterion.n_outer());
  u0(0) = 0.0;
  return laplace::engine::fit_marginal(criterion, u0);
}
```

## Accuracy and tests

The test suites `tests/engine/test_row_kernels.cpp` (4 test cases), `tests/engine/test_inner_fit.cpp` (9 test cases), and `tests/engine/test_marginal_criterion.cpp` (11 test cases) pass on both gcc and CUDA (see `docs/numerics.md`). All 34 logistic-curve derivative forms in `pointwise_node.hpp` are verified against SymPy at 50 digits within \f$10^{-40}\f$ (`tools/reference/engine_nodes.py`), and `packed_triple()` and `packed_quad()` are checked at compile time against `distrib::sym_tensor_position` for \f$n \in \{1, 2, 3, 4, 6\}\f$.

In `tests/engine/test_marginal_criterion.cpp`, central differences of \f$V\f$ and of \f$\nabla V\f$ at steps \f$h = 10^{-3}\f$ and \f$h/2\f$ (Richardson-extrapolated) start every evaluation from the inner minimum of the centre \f$u_0\f$. With polishing disabled, three cases (the Student \f$t\f$ prior, the shared hyperparameter, and the unstructured covariance) differ at \f$h = 10^{-3}\f$ by \f$10^{-7}\f$ because a decrement of \f$10^{-15}\f$ leaves an inner residual of about \f$10^{-8}\f$ in \f$x\f$ (entering \f$\frac{1}{2}\log|H|\f$ at first order through \f$\tau^\top d\f$), and the criterion of the frozen gamma reference fit (\f$V \approx 9178\f$) differs from the reference by \f$3.8 \times 10^{-8}\f$ in absolute value; with polishing enabled, every simulated case matches Richardson differences within \f$2.0 \times 10^{-10}\f$ (gradient) and \f$6.1 \times 10^{-11}\f$ (Hessian) on gcc, and within \f$5.7 \times 10^{-10}\f$ (gradient) and \f$1.2 \times 10^{-10}\f$ (Hessian) on CUDA, while that criterion matches the reference to \f$4.5 \times 10^{-11}\f$ in absolute value. On \f$10^6\f$ rows near the optimum, inner fits whose step fell below coordinate rounding before reaching decrement tolerance are accepted and counted in `inner_stalls`.

| Test suite and model | Reference or check | Tolerance |
|---|---|---|
| Row kernels: Gaussian (\f$n=300\f$, \f$G=10\f$, log link on \f$\sigma\f$) | Dense hand-written reference (value, gradient, observed, expected) | \f$10^{-12}\f$ of max scale |
| Row kernels: Gamma (\f$n=300\f$, \f$G=10\f$, logistic curve with log link) | Dense hand-written reference (value, gradient, observed, expected) | \f$10^{-12}\f$ of max scale |
| Row kernels: Gaussian with two crossed groupings (\f$n=300\f$, \f$G=6\f$ width \f$2\f$, \f$H=5\f$ width \f$1\f$) | Dense hand-written reference (value, gradient, observed, expected) | \f$10^{-12}\f$ of max scale |
| Row kernels: Student \f$t\f$ (\f$n=300\f$, separable) | Dense hand-written reference (value, gradient, observed, expected) | \f$10^{-12}\f$ of max scale |
| Row kernels: all four models | Value of every kernel and gradient of the three that give it vs `observed_information`; central differences (\f$h_j = 10^{-5}(1 + |x_j|)\f$) | \f$10^{-13}\f$ relative or of largest gradient entry; \f$10^{-7}\f$ of `scale + 1` |
| Inner fit: Gaussian with group prior (\f$n=2000\f$, \f$G=40\f$) | Dense vs sparse curvature point, gradient norm, cache reuse (`reused == 2`) | \f$10^{-7}\f$, \f$\|\nabla F\|_\infty \le 10^{-4}\f$ |
| Inner fit: Gaussian with cross-group random-walk penalty (\f$n=1500\f$, \f$G=15\f$) | Dense vs sparse value, gradient, Hessian, and fitted point | \f$10^{-14}\f$ rel. (value), \f$10^{-13}\f$ of scale (grad, Hess), \f$10^{-7}\f$ (point) |
| Inner fit: Gaussian with held coordinates (\f$n=1000\f$, \f$G=20\f$) | Exact preservation of held coordinates, gradient norm | exact, \f$\|\nabla F\|_\infty \le 10^{-4}\f$ |
| Inner fit: Gamma logistic curve (\f$n=3000\f$, \f$G=30\f$, sparse) | Stationarity and parameter recovery | \f$\|\nabla F\|_\infty \le 10^{-4}\f$ |
| Inner fit: Gaussian B-spline + group effect (\f$n=2000\f$, \f$G=20\f$) | Frozen reference inner-fit coordinates (`kokkos_fit_2000.csv`) | \f$10^{-9}\f$ absolute |
| Inner fit: Gamma logistic curve + asymptote (\f$n=2000\f$, \f$G=20\f$) | Frozen reference inner-fit coordinates (`lk3_gamma_2000.csv`) | \f$10^{-9}(1 + |x_{\mathrm{ref}, j}|)\f$ |
| Inner fit: Student \f$t\f$ (\f$n=3000\f$, dense) | Observed vs expected curvature point, gradient norm | \f$10^{-5}\f$, \f$\|\nabla F\|_\infty \le 10^{-4}\f$ |
| Inner fit: Lasso on 6 Gaussian location slopes (\f$n=2000\f$, `laplace1`, \f$s = 1/60\f$) | `row_model_composite_objective` (coordinate descent): optimality conditions (measured \f$2.5 \times 10^{-8}\f$ of \f$1/s\f$, 2 zeros, 1 eigenvalue correction) | \f$10^{-7} / s\f$ (nonzero), \f$(1 + 10^{-7}) / s\f$ (zero), \f$10^{-6}\f$ (unpenalized) |
| Inner fit: Group lasso on 6 Gaussian location slopes (\f$n=2000\f$, `mvpowerexp1` shape \f$1/2\f$) | `row_model_composite_objective` (FISTA): optimality condition (measured \f$7.7 \times 10^{-6}\f$ against \f$1/(2\sigma) = 50\f$ in 8 iterations) | \f$10^{-6} / (2\sigma)\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Gaussian (\f$n=400\f$, \f$G=10\f$, \f$\log\tau\f$ and scale coefficients) | Richardson differences (gcc error \f$1.3 \times 10^{-10}\f$ / \f$22\f$ grad, \f$1.6 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$735\f$ Hess) | \f$10^{-8}(1 + \text{scale})\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Gamma logistic curve (\f$n=400\f$, \f$G=10\f$, \f$\log\tau, \log\phi\f$) | Richardson differences (gcc error \f$4.0 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$62\f$ grad, \f$3.1 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$144\f$ Hess) | \f$10^{-8}(1 + \text{scale})\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Student \f$t\f$ (\f$n=300\f$, no penalty, \f$\log\nu\f$) | Richardson differences (gcc error \f$3.4 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$4.2\f$ grad, \f$2.7 \times 10^{-12}\f$ / \f$16\f$ Hess) | \f$10^{-8}(1 + \text{scale})\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Student \f$t\f$ prior (\f$n=400\f$, \f$\log\sigma, \log\nu\f$, 1 coeff.) | Richardson differences (gcc error \f$1.2 \times 10^{-10}\f$ / \f$60\f$ grad, \f$2.5 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$653\f$ Hess) | \f$10^{-8}(1 + \text{scale})\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Pseudo-Huber prior (\f$n=400\f$, \f$\log\sigma, \log\nu\f$) | Richardson differences (gcc error \f$5.7 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$0.24\f$ grad, \f$7.4 \times 10^{-12}\f$ / \f$10\f$ Hess) | \f$10^{-8}(1 + \text{scale})\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Shared \f$\tau\f$ across two predictors' effects (\f$n=500\f$, \f$G=12\f$) | Richardson differences (gcc error \f$9.6 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$14\f$ grad, \f$3.8 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$116\f$ Hess) | \f$10^{-8}(1 + \text{scale})\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Unstructured covariance over two predictors (\f$n=500\f$, log-Cholesky) | Richardson differences (gcc error \f$2.0 \times 10^{-10}\f$ / \f$8.2\f$ grad, \f$3.3 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$196\f$ Hess) | \f$10^{-8}(1 + \text{scale})\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Two crossed groupings (\f$n=600\f$, widths 2 and 1, 3 \f$\log\tau\f$, 1 coeff.) | Richardson differences (gcc error \f$1.6 \times 10^{-10}\f$ / \f$4.9\f$ grad, \f$5.0 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$115\f$ Hess) | \f$10^{-8}(1 + \text{scale})\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Covariance and precision forms (\f$n=400\f$, `mvgaussian1` vs `mvgaussian2`) | Richardson differences (gcc \f$7.3 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$112\f$, \f$6.1 \times 10^{-11}\f$ / \f$542\f$); agreement at opposite \f$\theta\f$ | \f$10^{-8}(1 + \text{scale})\f$; \f$10^{-10}\f$ rel. (\f$V\f$), \f$10^{-8}(1 + |\nabla V|_a)\f$ (grad) |
| Marginal criterion: Gaussian B-spline + group prior + scale (\f$n=2000\f$, 4 outer coordinates) | Frozen reference optimum (matches \f$V \approx 952\f$ within \f$9 \times 10^{-13}\f$ absolute; full fit from \f$u = 0\f$ within \f$3.8 \times 10^{-8}\f$ in 8 iterations) | \f$10^{-10}\f$ rel. (\f$V\f$), \f$\|\nabla V\|_\infty \le 10^{-5}\f$, \f$\|u - u_{\mathrm{ref}}\|_\infty \le 10^{-5}\f$ |
| Marginal criterion: Gamma logistic curve + asymptote (\f$n=2000\f$, 2 outer coordinates) | Frozen reference optimum (matches \f$V \approx 9178\f$ within \f$4.5 \times 10^{-11}\f$ absolute; full fit within \f$6 \times 10^{-8}\f$ in 5 steps) | \f$10^{-10}\f$ rel. (\f$V\f$), \f$\|\nabla V\|_\infty \le 10^{-5}\f$, \f$\|u - u_{\mathrm{ref}}\|_\infty \le 10^{-5}\f$ |

## Module functions and types

In namespace `laplace::engine`:
- Design blocks (`row_design.hpp`): \ref laplace::engine::design_entries "design_entries", \ref laplace::engine::design_column "design_column()", \ref laplace::engine::design_basis "design_basis()".
